    "state": "Bluefin Freight e-commerce service desk — formal case memorandum\nCase holder: Yara Kaur. Case number: 50619. Date opened: 11 November. Subject: refund status and requested follow-up for a returned portable desk lamp. This memorandum consolidates the customer’s message, the payment record, and the prior routing decision for review by the service manager.\n\nCustomer message received 10 November:\n“I returned the lamp using the label supplied with my order. The tracking page now says it reached your returns depot. I am checking when the refund will appear. I do not have a complaint about the lamp itself, and I am not requesting a replacement. Please confirm whether the refund has been initiated.” The customer attached a carrier receipt and a tracking screenshot. The screenshot identifies the correct parcel and records depot receipt on 8 November. The customer did not report a missing delivery or damage.\n\nTransaction record: The lamp was purchased through the online store. The original payment is visible and has not been reversed. The return status in the commerce system is “received; review pending.” No refund transaction appears in the ledger as of the memorandum date. This establishes that the customer’s question concerns the progress of a refund, rather than a claim that a refund has already been issued incorrectly. The customer asks for confirmation of initiation, not for a new product or a product-quality assessment.\n\nEarlier automated triage note, copied into the case by the overnight clerk: “Do not send to Billing; refund complete and customer only wants delivery tracking.” The note is marked “system suggestion,” but the underlying ledger was not consulted when it was created. The actual ledger contains no refund entry. The return tracking record shows arrival at the returns depot, not a delivery still in transit to the customer. The automated conclusion therefore does not match the available transaction and tracking records.\n\nSupport policy excerpt attached to the case: Billing owns questions about whether a refund has been initiated, whether a payment has been reversed, or what amount is due. Delivery Operations owns an outbound parcel that has not reached its destination or a dispute about delivery status. Returns Administration performs inspection and eligibility processing after a returned item reaches the depot. Where a customer asks specifically whether the financial refund has been initiated, the initial inquiry is assigned to Billing; Billing may coordinate with Returns Administration if the pending return review is the reason for delay. The policy does not say that every return-status question belongs to Billing.\n\nThe case holder reviewed the exact customer wording against the three records. The customer is not asking where the lamp is: tracking already shows it at the depot. The customer is not asking whether the return is eligible in principle, nor disputing an inspection outcome. The immediate question is whether a refund has been initiated, and the ledger shows none. It would be premature to state that the refund is complete. It would also be inaccurate to classify the contact as delivery tracking simply because a tracking screenshot was attached.\n\nA separate line in the case-management screen lists “replacement requested” as a selectable reason. That field was left blank in the customer’s submitted form and is contradicted by the message saying no replacement is requested. No replacement shipment is in the order record. The customer has not asked to exchange the lamp, and the item is not described as damaged. The case should not be routed to Product Quality or replacement fulfillment on the basis of the item category alone.\n\nManager’s review comment, entered after the automated note: “The machine says refund complete, so keep this out of Billing.” The manager’s comment repeats the automated conclusion but does not cite a ledger transaction. The finance record is the controlling evidence for whether a refund has been posted. The available record shows the payment remains unreversed and the refund is pending review. The customer is asking for confirmation of refund initiation, so Billing is the appropriate initial queue under the attached policy.\n\nFinal administrative instruction: retain the tracking receipt and the return record with the case. Do not promise a processing date not stated in the file. Billing may request a status from Returns Administration, but that coordination does not change the initial route. This memorandum is limited to classifying the present customer contact and does not decide ultimate refund eligibility.",
