    "state": "INCIDENT POSTMORTEM — Granite Rentals-sponsored evening course, “Safe Workshop Setup”\nPrepared by course operations after the 18 October session\n\nSummary\nA set of room and attendance details in the course portal was wrong for one evening. No one was injured, no equipment was lost, and no student was removed from the course. The incident began when a staff member copied the previous week’s room setup into the new session record without checking the instructor’s updated sheet. A later summary labeled the event “student cancellation,” although the evidence shows that the session went ahead as scheduled.\n\nTimeline and records\nAt 15:10, instructor Amara Alvarez submitted the updated plan: room G12, start time 18:00, and the standard safety briefing before equipment use. The course calendar already listed G12. At 15:42, operations assistant copied last week’s template, which listed G10 and a 17:30 setup slot. The template was not a current instruction; it was an archived record. At 16:05, Amara replied to the automated preview: “I am not cancelling the class. Please use G12 at 18:00; the students have already been told.” At 16:21, the portal nevertheless sent a notice displaying G10.\n\nStudents arrived at G12 at 17:55. The instructor checked the attendance list and began at 18:03, three minutes after the planned start. The short delay was caused by confirming the room and moving a portable sign. The course ran through the safety briefing and the scheduled practical demonstration. The signed attendance sheet lists twelve attendees. There is no note of a cancelled session, a cancelled enrollment, or a request to cancel a reservation.\n\nInitial automated incident label\n“Class cancelled by instructor; students should be offered a credit.”\n\nThis label was generated from the old template’s room field and the word “cancelling” in Amara’s message. It overlooks the full sentence, which says she is not cancelling, and the replacement instruction immediately following it. The label is therefore not a reliable account of what happened. The class took place, and no credit was promised or requested.\n\nImpact and response\nThe portal room notice caused several students to check with the front desk. Staff directed them to G12. One student arrived at G10 first, then walked to G12 before class began. That student was not late to the session. The instructor confirmed that the demonstration was completed. Operations corrected the portal room field at 18:12 and sent a corrected notice. The original start time remained 18:00; the actual start was 18:03. There was no charge adjustment, refund, or course credit.\n\nCorrective actions\nThe template will display its archived date prominently. Staff must compare the current instructor sheet with the preview before sending notices. The incident label will be changed from “class cancelled” to “incorrect room notice; class held.” The suggestion to add a second confirmation step is accepted. The proposal to refund all attendees is not applicable: the course was delivered, the students attended, and nobody requested money back.\n\nClosing observation\nThe incident was a room-data error, not a cancellation. A verb appearing inside a negated sentence is not evidence that the event described by that verb occurred. The portal has now been corrected, which is less exciting than the first label but more accurate.",
