elivered class, because the scheduled second meeting did not occur.\n\nFrom: Sera Nwosu\nDate: 4 February\n\nThe student also says that she never opened the materials kit and returned it to the front desk on 19 February. The intake clerk’s receipt identifies the sealed kit and course reference. The inventory clerk checked it the same afternoon and recorded “seal intact, returned to stock.” That is enough to treat the separately charged materials item under the rule, subject to confirming the amounts in the account export.\n\nFrom: Dmitri Lindqvist\nDate: 5 February\n\nAccount export: ceramics course fee, $240; materials kit, $30; unrelated art-history course fee, $180; art-history reader, $18. The art-history course began on 10 February, and Nia’s records show she continued attending; no refund request was made for that course. The student’s portal request is limited to ceramics. The account total across all four lines is $468, but the applicable decision should be based only on the requested course and its materials line.\n\nFrom: Sera Nwosu\nDate: 5 February\n\nOne detail for the file: our published rule says the 50% stage applies after the first class and before the second class. It does not say “scheduled class date”; the registrar confirmed that a cancelled meeting that was not taught is not a delivered class. The withdrawal timestamp is 18 February, and the school has no evidence of a ceramics class after 8 February. The request was not made before the first class, so a full course-fee refund is not supported. The 50% course-fee amount is $120. The sealed kit is separately refundable at $30 under the return condition, giving $150 in total. No refund is due for art history.\n\nForwarded note from billing supervisor, 5 February:\n“Verdict: Nia qualifies only for $120, because the second class was listed on the calendar before the withdrawal request. Do not refund the materials kit; students often say an item was sealed without returning it.” The supervisor’s not