    "state": "NORTHBRIDGE SCHOOL — CONTRACT CLAUSE WITH SIGNED AMENDMENTS\n\nAgreement title: Student Records Scanning and Course-File Delivery Services\nParties: Northbridge Evening School and Foxglove Freight\nEffective term: 1 January–30 June\n\nThe Provider will collect sealed course-file cartons from the school’s records office, transport them to the scanning centre, and return scanned files through the school’s secure portal. The school remains responsible for deciding which records may lawfully be included in a course file. The Provider may not release student records to instructors or outside parties unless the School’s records officer has approved the recipient. The services contract does not transfer ownership of school records or give the Provider authority to change enrollment or course-completion information.\n\nClause 3.1, delivery record. A carton is delivered to the scanning centre when a receiving employee signs the chain-of-custody receipt and records the carton identifier. A vehicle arrival time, dispatch entry, or automated “arrived nearby” alert is not by itself confirmation of delivery. If a carton is rejected because its seal is broken, the rejection and reason must be written on the receipt before the driver leaves. The Provider must preserve the signed receipt and provide a copy to the School on request.\n\nClause 5.2, service fee. The standard handling fee is $40 per accepted carton. The fee is payable only for cartons accepted at the centre and processed under the specification. A carton that is refused at intake is not an accepted carton. The School pays approved invoices within 15 calendar days after receipt of the supporting records. Additional storage is charged only where the School has expressly authorized it in a signed change order. Email proposals and draft comments do not amend this agreement.\n\nClause 7.4, incident and remedy. If the Provider loses or damages a carton while it is in the Provider’s custody, the Provider must report the incident promptly and pay a $75 service credit for the affected carton. A School employee’s internal handling before signed pickup is outside this remedy. The signed pickup receipt identifies when custody begins. A credit is due to the School, not an additional fee payable by the Provider to the School’s employee.\n\nAmendment A, signed 10 March. For cartons collected on or after 15 March, a special handling fee of $10 per accepted carton replaces the standard $40 fee for that carton. The total fee for an accepted carton covered by this amendment is therefore $10, not $50. All other provisions remain in effect. No special fee applies to cartons collected before 15 March. The amendment does not change the acceptance requirement or the incident remedy.\n\nAmendment B, signed 22 April. The school will permit one additional storage day without charge when the scanning centre rejects a carton for an unreadable label and the School supplies a corrected label by the next business day. This storage exception applies only to label rejections. A broken seal or missing chain-of-custody evidence does not qualify. This amendment does not make a rejected carton accepted and does not create a handling fee for it.\n\nShipment file, 24 April. Carton C-41, collected 18 March, carries a School records-office seal number 7742. The signed pickup receipt records the seal intact at 16:05 and names the Provider’s driver as taking custody. The centre’s signed intake receipt records C-41 accepted at 17:32 with the same intact seal and identifier. The carton was processed and returned through the portal. Carton C-42, collected 18 March, had a broken seal at pickup; the school clerk’s signed note says the break was observed before the driver accepted custody. C-42 was rejected, and no processing receipt exists. Carton C-43, collected 10 March, was accepted and processed before Amendment A’s effective collection date.\n\nInvoice submitted 25 April lists C-41 at $10, C-42 at $10, and C-43 at $40. It also lists a $75 service credit for C-42. No signed storage change order is attached. The invoice cover sheet says the total is “$60 due from the School after credit.” A dispatch analyst’s internal verdict states: “Provider owes the school $75 for the broken C-42 seal, and the school owes no handling fees because the seal was defective.” That verdict is inconsistent with the pickup and intake records: C-42’s seal was already broken before the Provider took custody, and the carton was refused. The analyst’s note is not a contractual amendment or an approved determination.\n\nSchool contract review, 26 April. C-41 is accepted after Amendment A’s effective date, so its $10 special fee applies and its documented accepted status satisfies the fee condition. C-42 was not accepted and the damage/incident provision does not apply because the broken seal was documented before Provider custody; the $10 fee and $75 credit are both unsupported. C-43 was accepted before the special-fee effective date, so the standard $40 fee applies. The supported handling charges total $50, with no supported service credit or storage charge. Finance has not yet approved the cover sheet total. The review asks the contract administrator to correct the invoice to the applicable contractual entries.",
