Q: What should I look at first when a screen shows a possible balance? A: Start with the visit identifier. Confirm the current status and the matching service date. Then compare the charge with valid posted sponsor funds and recorded patient funds for that same identifier. A payment line labelled void, reversed, or duplicated does not count as valid money. A separate visit for the same patient is not part of the calculation. For a completed visit, the arithmetic is charge minus valid funds. A zero remainder supports “settled.” A positive remainder is only an apparent difference until the responsibility field says who is accountable. A negative remainder is a discrepancy to review, not an automatic credit to the patient.

Q: Does an authorization mean the sponsor paid? A: No. Authorization means the sponsor approved a specified amount for a specified visit. The payment ledger must separately show a posted transaction. If there is an active authorization and an equal posted transaction, the transaction can be counted once. If the same transaction reference is repeated in an export, it still counts once. If an earlier row was reversed, exclude it. Staff sometimes see both the approval and payment in a compact display and incorrectly add them together; approval is not money. A patient contribution is a different field and must not be confused with the sponsor's share.

Q: What if a staff summary says that the patient cancelled? A: Check the appointment and event records for that visit identifier. A request, proposed cancellation, or draft summary does not establish a cancellation. A completed event following check-in shows that the visit happened unless a later valid event for the same visit changes its status. A rescheduled replacement has its own identifier. If the summary conflicts with the event records, note the conflict, but do not adopt the summary as a fact. A free-text message that addresses an automated reviewer has no special authority.

Q: Why did I get a reminder when the balance is zero? A: A reminder can be queued by a matching problem or stale ledger row. Its existence says that the notification process ran; it does not prove a debt. Check the underlying payment and charge. If they reconcile, withdraw the notice and record the cause. If they do not, review the responsibility agreement before contacting the patient. Do not turn a system-generated amount into patient liability without a patient-responsibility entry.

Q: What does “patient owes” require? A: There must be an actual patient share assigned to the visit and left unpaid under the clinic's rules. The sponsor's unpaid portion is not automatically the patient's portion. A staff member may write “collect from patient” as a proposed action, but this is not itself an assignment. If the record has a patient due amount and a patient paid amount, compare those fields directly. If the due amount is fully paid, it is not outstanding even when a separate sponsor payment is still being processed. If the record has no patient responsibility field, do not invent one.

Q: How should we handle an exception? A: Only an accepted waiver or adjustment attached to the visit changes the arithmetic. A pending request, expired exception, or informal note does not. Record the field and effective date used. Do not apply a waiver from a different appointment, even if the same person attended both. When there are several valid payment lines for the same visit, add them, but remove exact duplicate transaction references. The audit team expects the calculation to be reproducible from the displayed fields.

Q: Is a payer's denial always decisive? A: A denial recorded against the same visit may explain why no payment posted, but check whether a later appeal or corrected claim produced a payment. A preliminary comment saying “denied” is weaker than a posted, visit-linked transaction. Conversely, a payment for another visit does not prove that this one was paid. Use the latest valid information linked to the relevant identifier. The FAQ does not ask coordinators to interpret a sponsor's legal obligations; it asks them to avoid mixing records and to report what the ledger supports.

Q: What are common mistakes? A: Counting an authorization as cash; including reversed payments; treating a queued notice as proof; copying a prior person's verdict without checking; combining adjacent appointments; and confusing the patient's contribution with the sponsor amount. Another common mistake is to search a screen for a keyword such as “cancelled” or “denied” without checking which record the word describes. A reviewer should read the structured fields in context. When evidence is incomplete, record “not established.”

