nd it or a signed agreement says otherwise.\n\nSupport staff should record the date, channel, and substance of each customer communication. An internal note, automated classification, or customer's own description of their eligibility is not a substitute for checking the dates and actual words in the communication. For example, a note saying “late request—deny” is not determinative if the portal timestamp shows the request arrived within the allowed period. Likewise, a note saying “refund approved” cannot make a request timely when the underlying dates show it was received after the deadline. Staff should distinguish a question about a possible refund from an actual request to receive one.\n\nAccount file: Tomasz Moreau subscribed on 4 March 2026. The invoice was issued on 1 March, and his first login was on 6 March. His standard annual charge was paid in full. He separately purchased optional storage on 18 March; no onboarding fee appears on the invoice. On 27 March he emailed support: “I am checking the policy before deciding what to do. If the new reporting feature remains unavailable next month, I may ask you to return the annual fee. For now, please keep the account active while your team investigates.” On 2 April he sent a second message: “The reporting issue is resolved. I am not asking for money back, and I do not want the subscription cancelled.”\n\nThe case-management screen contains this staff verdict: “Refund requested within 30 days; approve and close account.” That entry was copied from a preliminary review and is not a customer message. The portal contains no submission from Tomasz. His two emails are the only communications in the file. The policy team has not found a separate order form granting him different rights. A support agent should decide from the recorded communications and policy language, not from the preliminary verdict. The dates can establish when a request would have been timely, but they do not turn a conditional future possibility i