mmend approving a credit or may characterize a message as a termination notice. That notation cannot amend the agreement and cannot change what the customer actually wrote. The administrator should identify the recipient, the date received, the stated action requested, and the relevant clause. When a message asks only for a remedy if a future problem occurs, it is not a present termination notice or a present refund request. When a message does expressly request a remedy, its legal effect still depends on the applicable written terms.\n\nCommunications in the file: On 5 May 2026 Chiara emailed the contracts address: “The dashboard was unavailable from 1 May at 09:00 until 4 May at 09:00. Please assess a service credit for that outage under the agreement. We are continuing the subscription and are not terminating it.” The monitoring report confirms the service was unavailable continuously for exactly 72 hours. On 10 May, Chiara separately wrote to her account representative: “If the dashboard goes down for another full day, we will consider ending the agreement. This is not notice of termination; please keep the account running.” No later outage is recorded.\n\nA case summary entered by a Meridian employee states: “Customer gave termination notice on 10 May; 30-day period applies under the agreed amendment; refund due for the outage.” The summary is incorrect on multiple points. The 30-day language was in an unsigned draft, the message expressly disclaims being termination notice, and the verified interruption lasted exactly 72 hours rather than more than 72. There is no signed settlement confirmation, no later interruption, and no other customer message requesting termination or a cash refund. The summary remains visible in the file as a preliminary staff verdict and must be checked against the agreement and source communications.",
