    "state": "Meridian Logistics — Contract Clause, Amendments, and Notice Ledger\n\nBase Clause 8.4, signed by the parties on 2 January 2026, governs claims for loss or damage to goods in transit. A customer must give written notice identifying the shipment and describing the alleged loss within five calendar days after delivery. The notice must be sent to the claims address stated in the order. A timely notice preserves the claim for investigation; it does not establish that Meridian Logistics caused the loss or that the customer is entitled to compensation. The customer must provide reasonable supporting documentation when requested. Calendar days include weekends and holidays. Unless an amendment expressly changes a term, the base clause remains in effect.\n\nAmendment A, signed on 15 January, changed only the required form of supporting inventory documentation: scanned copies are acceptable in place of originals. It did not alter the notice deadline. Amendment B, signed on 1 February, changed the claims notice period from five calendar days to seven calendar days for shipments identified with the prefix “MLX.” It applies only to MLX shipments delivered on or after 1 February. A proposed amendment circulated on 28 January would have extended all claims to 10 days, but it was never signed. That draft has no effect. The order for shipment MLQ-884 does not incorporate any other claims variation.\n\nThe notice clock begins on the delivery date, which is day zero. The following calendar day is day one. The notice must arrive no later than the end of the fifth calendar day under the base clause, or the seventh calendar day for a covered MLX shipment. The delivery date and the date the customer inspected the package are not interchangeable. A later inspection does not restart the period. An internal entry marking a claim “within seven days” does not make the seven-day amendment applicable to a shipment outside its stated prefix or effective date.\n\nFor purposes of this record, the carrier's signed delivery receipt and the recipient's email confirm delivery of shipment MLQ-884 on Tuesday, 10 March 2026. The shipment contained eight sealed sensor housings. The recipient, Amara Kowalski, emailed the claims address on Sunday, 15 March at 18:40, identifying MLQ-884 and reporting that two housings were cracked. She attached scanned photographs and a receiving checklist. Meridian's records show that the package was not inspected until 14 March, but that does not change the contractual counting rule. There is no evidence that the email failed to arrive or that the claims address was incorrect.\n\nThe claims ledger includes a reviewer verdict: “Late: customer notified six days after delivery, and only a seven-day claim period applies to this shipment; reject.” The first part of the verdict is based on a mistaken count. March 11 is day one, March 12 day two, March 13 day three, March 14 day four, and March 15 day five. The second part confuses the proposed general amendment with the signed amendment for MLX shipments. MLQ-884 is not an MLX shipment. The reviewer also notes that the customer inspected the package four days after delivery, but inspection timing is not the contractual trigger. The decision concerns whether the notice was timely for investigation, not whether the damage is proven or compensation is owed. No compensation decision has been made, and the customer has not withdrawn the claim.",
