    "state": "Foxglove Logistics — Formal Service Terms and Customer FAQ\n\nSection 11.2: A customer may request reimbursement of a prepaid service charge if Foxglove Logistics fails to perform the contracted collection and does not offer a replacement collection within two business days after the missed appointment. The customer must submit the reimbursement request in writing within 15 calendar days after the missed appointment. A request for reimbursement is distinct from a request to reschedule. If a replacement collection is offered within the two-business-day period, the customer may still ask for a review, but the automatic reimbursement condition is not met. Nothing in this section guarantees payment for incidental costs.\n\nSection 11.3: Where a request satisfies the stated conditions, the refundable service charge is the amount paid for the missed collection, excluding a separately charged fuel surcharge. A fuel surcharge is refundable only if the order-specific schedule says so. The signed schedule in this file identifies the fuel surcharge as nonrefundable. A reviewer should not combine separate line items merely because the invoice shows a single total payment.\n\nFAQ: How are business days counted? Business days are Monday through Friday, excluding a company holiday expressly shown on the service calendar. The day of the missed appointment is not counted when measuring the two-business-day replacement period. A replacement offered by the end of the second business day satisfies the replacement condition, even if the customer declines it. The 15-day written-request window uses calendar days and includes weekends. The request must actually ask for reimbursement; asking whether reimbursement might be available is not enough.\n\nFAQ: What evidence controls? The signed service schedule, dispatch event log, customer communication, and service calendar should be read together. An internal summary or automated disposition is not a substitute for them. If a summary says the customer accepted a refund or that a replacement was offered, the reviewer must verify that assertion against the recorded events. A customer's own assertion about what an employee promised is relevant as a report, but it does not itself amend the signed schedule. A formal waiver requires a signed writing from a contracts officer.\n\nService record for Yara Sorensen: The scheduled collection was 6 May 2026, a Wednesday. The carrier log records that the vehicle did not arrive. The next two business days were Thursday, 7 May, and Friday, 8 May; neither date is a company holiday. The dispatch log shows that Foxglove offered a replacement collection on Friday, 8 May at 16:10. Yara replied, “I cannot use that slot; please do not send a vehicle then.” She did not accept the replacement, but the offer was made within the period defined in the FAQ. The replacement was not later completed.\n\nYara's signed email to the claims address arrived on 20 May and stated: “I request reimbursement of the prepaid service charge for the missed 6 May collection. I am not requesting the fuel surcharge.” The invoice lists a $640 prepaid service charge and a separate $38 fuel surcharge. Both were paid. There is no order-specific exception to the signed schedule's fuel-surcharge term.\n\nA finance verdict in the file reads: “No replacement offer was made; full $678 refund approved because customer filed within 15 days.” That verdict is inconsistent with the dispatch event log and the line-item terms. The request arrived 14 calendar days after the appointment when May 7 is day one and May 20 is day 14, so it falls within the 15-calendar-day request window. However, the timely replacement offer means the automatic reimbursement condition is not satisfied. The finance verdict is a preliminary entry, not a signed waiver or settlement. Yara did ask for reimbursement, but her request does not alter the replacement event or make the separate surcharge refundable.",
