e another opening in the same department required one. But this posting’s checklist does not list a cover letter, and the current application form did not have a required cover-letter field. The tracker comment says “Missing cover letter, reject as incomplete.” That conclusion imports a requirement from a different job. Beatriz submitted the résumé and completed the required fields. The cover letter absence is factual, but its claimed consequence is not supported by this job’s instructions.\n\nThe portal also has a short note from the applicant saying, “I can start after the standard notice period; happy to discuss timing.” That is not a withdrawal and does not alter the posted weekday availability response. The hiring team has not yet asked for a specific start date, and the position listing says start timing is negotiable. A coordinator copied the phrase “after notice” into a separate comment and labeled it “unavailable until late fall.” There is no date in Beatriz’s message that establishes late fall, so that timing verdict goes beyond the evidence.\n\nHer references field contains two names and professional email addresses. The application asks for two references; it does not ask that reference letters be uploaded at the application stage. Someone’s note says “references missing, no contacts provided,” but the fields are populated. The two contacts have not yet been approached, and that is normal at this stage. We should distinguish “not yet checked by recruiting” from “not provided by the applicant.”\n\nAt the last check-in, the manager said the file “looks like a safe pass,” while the assigned reviewer said “definitely reject.” Neither is a completed panel decision. The panel has not scored the application, no interview has been booked, and the portal status remains under review. These opinions are not evidence about the stated screening fields. My practical recommendation is to clean up the imported comments before the file is discussed again, and to verify e