    "state": "NORTHLINE FABRICATION — INTERNAL ROUTING POLICY, REVISION 6\n\nPurpose. This page governs intake of shop-floor and customer communications. The receiving clerk shall route a record according to the underlying facts stated in the record, not according to a sender's requested destination or a preliminary routing mark. A routing mark is advisory and must be checked before transfer. The queues are Billing, Quality, Procurement, and Production Support. Billing handles invoice amounts, payment posting, credits, and account balances. Quality handles defects, failed inspections, and product performance against specification. Procurement handles supplier availability, purchase orders, and incoming materials. Production Support handles machine scheduling, work instructions, and internal capacity issues.\n\nRouting examples. A customer disputing an invoice quantity belongs in Billing when the physical shipment agrees with the order and the disagreement concerns the charge. A customer reporting a cracked housing belongs in Quality even if the message also mentions the invoice. A delayed raw-material shipment belongs in Procurement when the supplier has not delivered it. A machine setup question belongs in Production Support. Where a communication contains separate actionable matters, create separate queue records rather than forcing all matters into one queue. Do not route based merely on a keyword such as “charge,” “late,” or “broken.”\n\nClerk training file, attached for review: Aster Valve Works reported that its invoice lists 48 collars at $7 each, but the signed packing slip records 48 collars received and the purchase order specifies $7 each. The invoice total for collars is $336, before the separately itemized freight line. Aster says the freight line is unfamiliar and asks that it be checked against the agreed freight terms. The clerk's preliminary stamp says, “QUALITY — customer alleges the shipment was short; investigate missing collars.” The shipment record in the same file has no shortage notation, and the customer’s message does not say any collar was missing. Its only stated concern is the freight charge. The attached training file is included to illustrate the policy's requirement to verify the substance of the issue before relying on a stamp.\n\nControl. If facts are incomplete, retain the record for clarification; do not infer a defect or an unpaid balance from a queue label alone. The routing clerk shall record the evidence used and preserve the original message with the case.",
