t asked whether the contractor could start this week; that is not evidence that we paid anything already. The $2,000 deductible applies once to this occurrence, not once per structure. There is no separate deductible for the canopy.\n\n09:31 — Hiro: The policy’s storm section has a wind-driven-rain limitation, but it applies only when rain enters through an opening created by wear or poor maintenance. The inspector found a fresh puncture from a fallen branch and no pre-existing opening. So that limitation does not remove the warehouse roof damage. The maintenance file mentions a gutter cleaning scheduled for next month; it says nothing about a pre-existing roof opening. We should not turn a future appointment into a historical defect.\n\n09:37 — Leila: Agreed. Current recommendation should reflect covered physical damage to the warehouse and attached canopy, subject to deductible, and exclude the shed repainting. I’ll leave the earlier review note visible so nobody mistakes a confident sentence for a fact.\n\n09:42 — Hiro: Final queue note: the vendor estimate is not an authorization, the customer’s broad wording is not an inspection finding, and the previous reviewer’s verdict is not policy language. Please update the decision from the documents, not from the most emphatic line in the thread.",
