    "state": "Meeting notes — Foxglove Software business-interruption claim review\n\nAttendees: Nadia Alvarez, claims examiner; Sol Renn, forensic accountant; Bram Eddow, broker representative. The meeting was called to review the interruption calculation after a cooling failure at Foxglove Software’s leased server room. No one is disputing that the cooling unit failed on 6 August or that operations were interrupted. The point of this meeting, naturally, is what the policy actually pays for.\n\nNadia opened with the policy wording. The relevant endorsement covers lost net income during the period of restoration, up to a maximum of 14 consecutive calendar days. The period begins when the covered physical loss causes suspension and ends when the equipment is repaired or could reasonably have been repaired. The repair log shows the replacement cooling unit was operational on 17 August at 18:00. The suspension began on 6 August at 18:00. That is 11 elapsed days, so the 14-day cap does not shorten the documented period. There is no separate waiting period in this endorsement.\n\nSol summarized the accounting schedule. The submitted total is $96,000: $70,000 in lost operating margin, $16,000 in ordinary payroll, and $10,000 in new emergency cloud-hosting expense. The policy defines covered lost net income as lost revenue minus saved variable expenses. Sol reconciled the relevant records and found $70,000 in lost operating margin after saved expenses. Ordinary payroll continued during the interruption and is not an additional covered expense under this endorsement. The cloud-hosting invoices total $10,000 and were reasonable extra expenses incurred to keep customer services available; the endorsement includes reasonable extra expense. The supported covered amount before any applicable limit is thus $80,000, not the $96,000 submitted total.\n\nBram read the prior desk note aloud: “The entire $96,000 schedule is supported and payable; payroll is part of interruption loss.” He then said it was reassuring to see a number in quotation marks. Nadia pointed out that the policy’s definition, payroll records, and vendor invoices contradict that conclusion. The $16,000 payroll was paid as usual, but no wording in this coverage makes it an extra covered item. This is not a judgment about whether payroll was sensible; it is a classification under this endorsement.\n\nThe team checked whether the cloud vendor’s charges were already included in the operating-margin calculation. Sol confirmed they were not: the $70,000 figure is lost margin only, and the $10,000 vendor invoices are separately itemized. The invoices match the dates of the outage and were paid by Foxglove. The claim file contains a separate $4,000 estimate for replacing a damaged network switch, but that item is in the property-damage section and is not part of this interruption schedule. Nobody should add it to, or subtract it from, the business-interruption calculation.\n\nNadia also reviewed the claimant’s proposed $5,000 goodwill credit to customers. It appears in an email draft but was never issued. No ledger entry or payment record supports it as an incurred expense. The customer credits are therefore not included in the business-interruption amount. Bram noted that the company expects some customers to leave next quarter; Nadia said future expectations are not incurred costs or covered losses during the recorded interruption.\n\nConclusion: the interruption lasted 11 elapsed days, within the 14-calendar-day maximum. The submitted schedule contains supported lost margin and extra expense, but also ordinary payroll that the endorsement does not cover. The earlier desk note is preserved for audit purposes, not adopted as the decision. The team will calculate payment using the supported $80,000 amount and the applicable policy terms.",
