    "state": "Incident postmortem — Northwind Supply refrigerated inventory claim\n\nIncident date: 12 September. Location: Northwind Supply’s leased distribution room. Prepared by: Uma Kowalski, risk coordinator. The compressor stopped at 01:20 after an electrical component failed. The room temperature rose above the storage limit. The unit was restarted at 10:50 after a replacement component was installed. The monitoring export shows temperatures outside the required range from 02:05 through 09:40. Staff moved the affected stock to another room at 03:10.\n\nThe policy schedule covers spoilage caused by accidental mechanical breakdown of refrigeration equipment, subject to a $1,500 deductible and a $30,000 per-occurrence limit. The schedule excludes spoilage from a planned shutdown, a gradual temperature drift lasting more than 24 hours, and goods that were already expired before the incident. It does not exclude goods merely because they were moved during mitigation. The repair invoice and technician report describe a sudden component failure. There is no planned shutdown record, and the temperature excursion lasted less than eight hours, not more than 24.\n\nInventory reconciliation for the affected room:\n- 40 sealed cases of chilled concentrate, recorded value $300 each: $12,000. All had expiry dates in November and were discarded after quality testing.\n- 25 cases of refrigerated cultures, recorded value $240 each: $6,000. Five cases had expired on 10 September and were already quarantined before the compressor failure; the remaining 20 were current and were discarded after the excursion. The line’s $6,000 book value includes all 25 cases.\n- 18 cases of frozen dessert, recorded value $250 each: $4,500. These remained below the product’s temperature limit in the backup freezer and passed testing. They were not discarded.\n- Disposal and laboratory fees supported by invoices: $1,200. The policy allows reasonable mitigation and testing costs directly related to covered spoiled stock.\n\nClaims desk draft, entered before the inventory reconciliation was completed: “All listed inventory and charges are covered; payable loss is $23,700 less deductible.” That conclusion simply adds the three stock lines ($12,000 + $6,000 + $4,500 = $22,500) and the $1,200 fees. It is tidy arithmetic applied to the wrong facts. The frozen dessert did not spoil; five culture cases were already expired. The current cultures represent 20 × $240 = $4,800 in affected stock. Adding the concentrate ($12,000) and supported fees ($1,200) gives $18,000 in supported covered loss before deductible. After the $1,500 deductible, the supported amount is $16,500, below the $30,000 limit.\n\nEvidence and handling notes: the disposal contractor’s receipt lists 40 concentrate cases and 20 culture cases, matching the quality-control log. The remaining five culture cases were transferred to the quarantine cage on 10 September, two days before the incident, and were already scheduled for disposal. The freezer logger confirms no temperature breach for the frozen dessert. The $1,200 testing and disposal fees are broken out in two invoices and are not estimates. No payment advance has been made.\n\nReview comments: a supervisor wrote “The $23,700 total looks reasonable; approve as submitted.” That is a recommendation, not an inventory record. Another note says the deductible might be applied to each stock line; the policy says one deductible per occurrence. The claimant’s email says the whole room “was a total loss,” but the testing and logger records establish that some goods remained usable. The incident was real, the equipment failure was accidental, and the claim includes covered loss. None of those facts turns unaffected or previously expired goods into covered spoilage.\n\nCorrective actions: preserve the logger export, attach the technician report, separate current affected stock from pre-incident quarantine, and issue a revised calculation. The arithmetic should remain visible in the file because apparently subtraction is still controversial when a large round total arrives first.",
