    "state": "Meeting notes — Meridian Studio water-damage claim\n\nParticipants: Yara Tanaka, property examiner; Ellis Mar, building consultant; Juno Pike, broker. Purpose: review source, ownership, and valuation of items listed after a pipe leak in Meridian Studio’s rented suite. The leak occurred on 4 February when a supply fitting failed inside the suite wall. The building owner’s maintenance log shows no prior leak at that location. The insured reported the incident the same morning and moved equipment away from the wet area.\n\nPolicy extracts read into the minutes: Meridian Studio’s business property coverage applies to equipment it owns or is legally responsible for, subject to a $1,000 deductible. The building owner’s fixtures are not Meridian’s business property. Property belonging to clients is covered only when specifically scheduled; the schedule attached to this policy lists client artwork, but not client computers. The policy pays actual cash value, defined here as replacement cost less documented depreciation. Reasonable emergency drying costs are covered. The policy does not cover upgrades or improvements that were not damaged by the event.\n\nSubmitted schedule and evidence:\n1. Meridian-owned design workstation: replacement cost $8,000; purchase record from three years ago; depreciation rate in the appraisal is 25%; water exposure and failure documented by the technician. Actual cash value is $8,000 − (0.25 × $8,000) = $6,000.\n2. Client-owned laptop belonging to a visiting contractor: replacement cost $2,400; water exposure documented. The client-property schedule contains two named paintings and no computers. Meridian has no lease or contract making it responsible for this laptop.\n3. Building owner’s wall-mounted control panel: repair estimate $1,700. The building inventory identifies it as an owner fixture. It failed during inspection, but the consultant found corrosion predating the leak and no water entry at the panel.\n4. Emergency drying and dehumidifier invoice: $900, paid by Meridian, dated the day of the loss. The drying contractor’s report ties the work to the wet suite.\n5. Proposed replacement of unaffected shelving with taller custom shelving: $3,100. The shelving remained dry and functional; the proposal describes an improvement to increase storage.\n\nAt the start of the meeting, the intake reviewer’s note was displayed: “All five listed items are eligible; replacement values total $16,100.” Juno observed that the total was at least easy to remember. Yara explained that the note conflates ownership, scheduled property, actual cash value, and mere proposals. It is not enough for an amount to appear on a claimant’s spreadsheet. The workstation’s eligible value is $6,000, not its $8,000 replacement cost. The drying invoice is a covered emergency expense. The laptop is client-owned and not scheduled; the panel is the building owner’s fixture and the evidence also places its corrosion before the event; the shelving was not damaged.\n\nThe parties also checked the deductible. It applies once to the covered occurrence, not separately to equipment and drying. The currently supported covered amount before deductible is $6,900 ($6,000 workstation plus $900 drying). Applying the $1,000 deductible would leave $5,900, subject to the remaining policy terms. The insurer has not yet issued payment. The contractor’s laptop owner has a separate policy, but that fact neither creates coverage under Meridian’s contract nor changes the submitted item’s ownership.\n\nThe final minutes record that the leak itself is covered in principle and that the workstation damage and emergency drying are supported. The desk note’s blanket approval is retained to show why item-level review matters. No participant endorsed paying for the client’s unscheduled laptop, an owner fixture, or dry shelving merely because they appeared in the same packet.",
