    "state": "Subject: The bracket order, the refund question, and the ever-growing reply-all\n\nFrom: Zoltan Sorensen, shop floor\nTo: the little production crew\n\nMorning all. I am putting this here because the bracket order has somehow become three conversations, and I can no longer tell which one is the real one. The original order from Meridian Rentals was for 24 steel wall brackets, powder-coated blue, due on the 18th. We cut and packed them on the 16th. Nothing dramatic there, except that the first packing slip had the old drawing revision in its header. The parts themselves were made to revision C, which is what the signed order specified. The slip was corrected before the boxes went out, and the corrected copy is in the job folder.\n\nA note in the folder from the person who opened the complaint says, “Customer wants their money back because we shipped the wrong revision.” That sentence has already been repeated in the morning huddle, so I want to be fussy about what the customer actually said. Their email arrived on the 20th: “We see the header on the first slip says rev B. Can you confirm the physical pieces match rev C? Please don’t start a refund; we need the parts for the installation, and the corrected paperwork is enough if the pieces match.” We sent photographs of the stamped part number, and their buyer replied, “Confirmed, those are the C parts. Thanks for checking.”\n\nThere was another small issue, which may be where the refund story came from. The courier charged us a re-label fee after the original slip was replaced. The fee was $18. It is on our shipping account, not on Meridian’s invoice. Meridian paid the invoice in full on the 19th. They did not ask us to return that payment, reduce it, or credit it against the next order. The accounting note says “refund exposure: $18,” but that is our internal estimate of the courier fee, not a request from the buyer.\n\nReply from Esi, 9:12: “I heard the buyer say refund on the phone.”\n\nReply from Zoltan, 9:20: “I called them back. They said, ‘I did not ask for a refund; I asked whether the paperwork could be fixed.’”\n\nReply from Esi, 9:23: “Ah, I was standing by the saw and only caught the word refund from the other end. Sorry.”\n\nReply from the shipping desk, 9:41: “The automated complaint category was Refund / Wrong Item. I did not choose it. The portal selected that category after the first slip was uploaded.”\n\nThe portal’s auto-summary reads: “Customer requests complete reimbursement for incorrect goods.” That is not a quote from Meridian. It was generated after the portal saw the words ‘wrong revision’ in the internal complaint text. The buyer’s own message is above, and the physical parts were later accepted. I am not saying the paperwork mistake was imaginary; it was real and needed correcting. I am saying the paperwork issue and a request for money back are not the same thing.\n\nLater in the chain, Mateo wrote: “If the customer asks for a refund later, we should route it through finance.” That is a future condition, not evidence that they have asked. Mateo also pasted a draft reply saying, “We are happy to refund you.” The draft was never sent, and he clarified that he was offering a possible response if the buyer chose that option. The buyer did not choose it.\n\nAt the end of the day, the corrected slip was acknowledged, the invoice remained paid, and the buyer wrote: “Thanks, we are all set; please keep the order active as shipped.” The production board still has a red refund flag because nobody has cleared the portal category. I can clear it once we decide whether the flag or the actual email is the useful source. Please do not mistake a portal label, a proposed response, or a coworker’s first impression for the buyer’s request. The physical goods were accepted, and there is no message asking for repayment.",
