    "state": "Meeting notes, Tuesday, 14:00–15:05, back table by the tool cabinet. Present: Esi Tanaka, Oren Haddad, two operators, and the person taking notes. We were supposed to review the small run of pump collars for Larkspur Analytics, but, as usual, the first ten minutes went to finding the right drawing and arguing about whether the coffee machine has been making a new noise or the same old noise with a different rhythm.\n\nThe order is for 40 collars, aluminum, 22 millimeters inside diameter, with a tolerance written on the signed drawing. The order sheet calls for a matte finish. The customer’s inspection sheet asks us to record the measured diameter for each piece, not merely the sample average. We did record all 40. The table is in the quality folder; the meeting did not reproduce every measurement because we would have spent the whole hour reading numbers aloud. The lowest recorded diameter is 21.96 mm and the highest is 22.04 mm. The drawing allows 21.90 through 22.10 mm, inclusive. No part falls outside the stated band.\n\nThe first lot of blanks had a surface mark on six pieces. They were not shipped. We set those aside, ran replacement blanks, and inspected the final group. The customer received 40 finished pieces, not 46. This matters because a draft note says “six nonconforming collars sent to customer,” which has been causing an unnecessary amount of whispering near the packing bench. The six marked blanks were scrapped before finishing, and their serial labels were voided. The finished shipment’s serial list contains 40 entries.\n\nOren read the draft quality summary aloud: “The batch is over tolerance and should be treated as a failed inspection.” That wording is not supported by the inspection table. When asked where “over tolerance” came from, Oren said he had copied a comment from the first setup sheet, which was written before the machine offset was adjusted. The setup sheet showed an early reading of 22.13 mm. That was on the test piece, not a shipped collar. The offset was corrected, the test piece was discarded, and the final measurements are the figures above. Oren agreed the summary should not describe the shipped batch as failed.\n\nWe also looked at finish. The signed order says matte. The customer’s email from before production says, “A low-gloss satin is acceptable if matte coating is unavailable.” Matte coating was available and was applied to all 40. A note from the finishing lead says, “Customer accepted glossy surface.” That is another inaccurate summary: there is no glossy finish on the shipped pieces, and the customer’s conditional allowance did not mean gloss was required or selected. The coating card records matte, and the customer’s receiving photo looks consistent with that card.\n\nEsi said, “I’m not calling this a complaint just because someone used the word defect in a draft. Let’s separate what the customer said from what we said about what the customer said.” We found a brief customer email after delivery: “We have received the package. We have not found a dimensional issue so far. Please send the inspection record when convenient.” That message requests the record, not replacement parts or money. A later note in the customer-service tracker says “buyer alleges all parts defective.” The tracker entry was created by a new clerk who was looking at the setup-sheet test reading and the draft summary, rather than the receiving email.\n\nDiscussion wandered to the number of boxes. The shipping label says two cartons; an older dispatch screen says one. Two cartons went out because the padding was split between them. The total piece count remained 40. Neither carton held the discarded test piece or the six rejected blanks. The courier’s scan records two cartons delivered, and the customer acknowledged one delivery event. This mismatch between carton count and box count in the old screen is not a product-quality finding.\n\nOren asked whether we should tell the customer that every measurement was within tolerance. Esi said yes, attach the measurement table, but do not imply they complained about dimensions. The customer has not sent a separate inspection report or identified a bad serial number. The customer’s exact wording is above. We should answer what they asked—send the record—and avoid turning our internal draft into a customer accusation.\n\nAction items: the clerk will amend the tracker to remove “all parts defective”; Oren will replace “failed inspection” with a factual statement that the shipped pieces’ recorded diameters were within the drawing range; the packing lead will keep the two-carton scan with the job file; and Esi will send the requested table. Nobody voted to issue a credit, scrap the shipped set, or remake the order. We did not decide those things because the evidence presented does not support them. The meeting ended with a promise to label the setup test piece more clearly next time, so it cannot be confused with finished production again.",
