    "state": "Subject: Forwarding the punch press notes, because the maintenance board is telling a more dramatic story than the machine\n\nOne-line cover note from Mateo Rahman: “The board says the operator broke the press by running it dry; below is the complete mail chain, including what the operator and service tech actually observed.”\n\nForwarded thread, Tuesday morning\n\nFrom: shop supervisor\nTo: maintenance desk\n\nPress 2 stopped during the second batch of spacer tabs. The board has been updated to “operator damage—ran without lubricant.” Please advise whether we need to hold the remaining jobs. There are 180 tabs on the job card, 120 already completed, and 60 not started. The press stopped before the last 60 were run. We have not scrapped the 120 completed tabs; they are in the inspection queue.\n\nFrom: Aiden Alvarez, operator\nTo: shop supervisor\n\nI did not run the press dry. The oil-level sight glass was at the marked line when I checked before the first batch, and I added no oil because it did not need any. The machine began making a tapping sound about halfway through the second batch. I stopped it, hit the normal stop, and called the supervisor. I did not restart it. The last 60 pieces were not made. I am attaching the start-of-shift photo of the gauge and the stop-time sheet.\n\nFrom: shop supervisor\nTo: Aiden Alvarez\n\nThe incident form has “no oil” checked. Did you mean that you found no oil, or that you did not add oil?\n\nFrom: Aiden Alvarez\nTo: shop supervisor\n\nI meant I did not add oil because the level was already at the line. I did not report an empty reservoir. The form wording is ambiguous, and I can correct it.\n\nFrom: maintenance technician\nTo: shop supervisor, Aiden Alvarez\n\nI checked Press 2 at 11:40. The reservoir is above the minimum mark. The feed line is open and oil is reaching the fitting. The tapping sound appears to come from a worn guide bearing. The bearing is not part of the routine lubrication system. I have not yet dismantled it, so this is a preliminary finding, but I see no sign of a dry-running seizure. The press must remain stopped until the bearing is replaced and alignment checked.\n\nFrom: shop supervisor\nTo: production list\n\nCorrection to the board note: I selected “ran without lubricant” after reading “no oil” on the incident form. I did not ask what that meant before posting. The operator’s statement and the technician’s inspection do not support that conclusion. We should record the cause as “probable guide-bearing wear; under inspection,” not operator misuse.\n\nThe maintenance board export still shows a large red warning: “Confirmed operator-caused failure.” That label was generated from the earlier board entry and did not change when the supervisor sent the correction. It is not a separate finding. The machine has a maintenance history of two bearing replacements, one 11 months ago and one 27 months ago. Neither history entry says it was caused by lubrication failure. A small note from last month says the press vibrated briefly after a tool change, but the vibration ended when the tooling was reseated. That earlier event is not evidence that this operator ran the machine dry today.\n\nFrom: maintenance technician, 13:10\nTo: shop supervisor\n\nI have now removed the guide cover. The bearing has uneven wear and a chipped edge. The oil passage is clear, and the contact surfaces are not heat-scored. This supports the preliminary view that the bearing failed from wear, though I cannot determine exactly when the chip formed. The press was stopped promptly after the sound began. I recommend replacing the bearing and inspecting the alignment before returning it to service.\n\nFrom: production planner\nTo: maintenance desk\n\nDoes this mean all 180 tabs are unusable?\n\nFrom: quality lead\nTo: production planner\n\nNo. The 120 completed pieces have not yet been inspected, so we should not declare them conforming or defective from the machine event alone. We can inspect them against the drawing. The unstarted 60 are simply not produced yet. The customer was told the shipment could be one day late if the press repair takes longer than planned. We have not said that the customer caused a delay, and the customer has not requested cancellation or repayment.\n\nFrom: supervisor, later that afternoon\nTo: all\n\nI have corrected the incident form. The check box now says “oil level checked; no oil added because level was adequate.” I have also removed “confirmed operator-caused failure” from the live board. The export remains in the archive with its earlier wording, so please don’t use that stale line as the final diagnosis. The technician’s inspection is the best evidence we have right now. We may learn more after the bearing supplier examines the chipped edge, but that could change the precise wear history, not the evidence that the operator checked the oil and stopped when the press made an unusual sound.\n\nSomeone asked whether the customer had been told that the finished tabs were bad. No: the message sent to the customer says only that maintenance is underway and the dispatch date may shift by a day. The customer replied, “Understood, send the inspection result with the shipment.” The customer did not accuse the operator or ask for compensation. A draft in the service mailbox says “We accept responsibility for damaged tabs,” but it was not sent and was written before the quality inspection. It should not be mistaken for a factual statement about the 120 completed parts.\n\nThis is why we are trying to keep the maintenance record, quality record, and customer correspondence separate. A machine stopping is a fact. A person being blamed is an interpretation, and in this case the first interpretation was copied into a bright red field before anyone clarified the form. The later correction and the technician’s physical findings should be used when answering questions about what happened.",
