    "state": "Meeting notes, Friday, 8:30 a.m., beside the packing tables. Present: Oren Haddad, the two people from finishing, the person running the small press, and the note-taker. This meeting was meant to be short, which is a charming goal we managed to miss by nearly an hour. The job under discussion is a batch of 30 hinge pins for a local equipment repair outfit. The customer is not named in the production copy, so we will call them “the buyer” here. The pins were made from the supplied stock and measured after turning. The drawing calls for a length of 48 mm, with an allowed range of 47.5–48.5 mm.\n\nThere are 30 final pins. The inspection sheet has 30 readings, from 47.7 mm to 48.3 mm. A preliminary machine setup record has one reading of 48.8 mm, but it is marked “test cut—before adjustment.” That test piece was not included in the 30-piece batch and was discarded. A note on the job traveler says “five pins too long; send back to lathe.” That note was entered before the adjustment and was never crossed out. It looks like a live production result, but the inspector confirmed that the final sheet is for the finished batch only and that no final pin exceeded 48.5 mm.\n\nThe packing issue is easier to explain than the paper trail suggests. The order requested 30 pins, and the shipment record says 30. The customer’s first email after delivery says, “We received the box and counted 30. Please send the material certificate when you can.” There is no mention of a short count or a failed dimension in that email. The customer did later ask whether the pins were made from the alloy listed on the order. The certificate shows that alloy. The buyer’s message says, “We are asking for confirmation, not alleging a material substitution.”\n\nThe service tracker has a red entry: “Customer reports wrong alloy and five overlength parts; requests remake.” Oren brought that up at the start of the meeting and said we should prepare a remake quote. After reading the actual messages, he said, “That tracker line is doing too much. It joined the stale traveler note to the customer’s alloy question and turned both into a complaint.” Oren’s first conclusion was therefore not the final account. The buyer’s words and the inspection sheet are the evidence we should use.\n\nA separate email from the buyer’s maintenance technician says: “One pin feels tight in the hinge we are repairing. We have not measured it yet, so it may be our hinge bore rather than the pin. Could you send the dimension record so we can compare?” That is a question and a possible explanation, not a report that the pin is out of specification. The quality lead has not received the hinge itself and cannot test the fit. We should not pretend the customer found a defect when they have explicitly said they have not measured the pin.\n\nThe meeting got sidetracked by a discussion of whether “feels tight” should count as a complaint. We agreed it is a fit concern worth responding to, while remaining careful about its cause. The buyer has not asked us to refund the order, replace all 30 pins, or stop using the material. They asked for a certificate and a dimension record. Those documents were attached to the reply sent at 10:05. The buyer answered, “Thanks. We will check our bore and come back if there is still a concern.” No further message has arrived.\n\nThe shipping label mentions two parcels. The job card says one box. In practice, the pins were divided into two padded cartons because the first carton was too heavy for the shelf scale; the combined count is still 30. There was no second shipment and no missing carton. The carrier’s first scan logged parcel one; the second scan for parcel two arrived seven minutes later. The customer confirmed receiving the package, singular, but that phrase does not tell us how many cartons were inside. We have the two scans and the signed delivery record.\n\nThe finishing lead also said the coating note looked odd. There was no coating on this job; the pins were supplied bright-turned. A template line about “matte black” copied over from the previous job but was crossed out on the signed traveler. The customer did not ask about color. It would be a mistake to conclude that the wrong finish was supplied simply because the template line remains visible in the scanned page.\n\nAction items: update the traveler so the discarded setup piece cannot be confused with the final inspection; correct the tracker to say the customer asked for material confirmation and records; and ask the buyer to send a measured pin or hinge only if their comparison still shows a fit concern. The meeting did not decide that the customer’s equipment is faulty either. We simply do not have enough evidence to assign the tight fit to either side. The dimensions we do have are within the drawing range, and the customer’s own message says they have not yet measured the part.\n\nOren closed by saying, “I would rather have a boring accurate record than an exciting wrong one.” Someone wrote this down, which is probably the most useful sentence from the hour. The old red tracker label remains in the archived export, but it should not override the actual messages, production inspection, or later correction.",
